WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.861
This video explains the Labour Force
Survey release for the second quarter

00:00:04.861 --> 00:00:08.086
of 2025 and the definitions behind
its headline figures.

00:00:09.436 --> 00:00:13.180
Every quarter, the National
Statistics Office interviews a sample

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.693
of households about the work of each
member aged 15 and over.

00:00:17.443 --> 00:00:21.142
The answers are weighted so that the
sample represents the whole

00:00:21.142 --> 00:00:22.471
population of that age.

00:00:23.221 --> 00:00:27.547
The office then publishes a release
with a table of headline figures and

00:00:27.547 --> 00:00:29.350
a report on the labour market.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.964
A journalist opens the new release
and wants to know whether

00:00:33.964 --> 00:00:36.100
unemployment fell during the quarter.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:38.930
The table answers in one line.

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:43.782
The unemployment rate was 6.1 percent
in the second quarter, after 6.3

00:00:43.782 --> 00:00:45.238
percent in the first.

00:00:45.988 --> 00:00:49.449
The table also marks the
second-quarter rate with the letter

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:52.102
P, which means that the figure is
provisional.

00:00:53.252 --> 00:00:56.942
A rate shown without its definition
is easy to misread.

00:00:56.942 --> 00:01:01.639
One reader takes 6.1 percent as the
share of all adults without a job.

00:01:02.389 --> 00:01:06.153
A second reader sets the overall rate
beside the youth rate

00:01:06.153 --> 00:01:08.195
and reads the two as one series.

00:01:08.945 --> 00:01:12.296
A third reader quotes the provisional
figure months later,

00:01:12.296 --> 00:01:14.145
after the office has revised it.

00:01:15.295 --> 00:01:19.210
The survey first sorts every person
into one of three groups for the

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:23.010
reference week: employed, unemployed,
or outside the labour force.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.136
A person counts as employed after
working at least one hour for pay or

00:01:28.136 --> 00:01:31.824
profit in that week, or when
temporarily absent from a job.

00:01:32.974 --> 00:01:37.329
A person without work counts as
unemployed after looking for work in

00:01:37.329 --> 00:01:38.610
the last four weeks.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.684
The person must also be able to start
a job now.

00:01:42.434 --> 00:01:45.791
These definitions follow the
international standard of the

00:01:45.791 --> 00:01:50.015
International Labour Organization, so
that the rates can be compared with

00:01:50.015 --> 00:01:51.462
those of other countries.

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:56.098
The labour force is the employed and
the unemployed together.

00:01:56.848 --> 00:02:00.434
The unemployment rate divides the
number of unemployed persons

00:02:00.434 --> 00:02:01.591
by the labour force.

00:02:02.341 --> 00:02:06.444
The participation rate divides the
labour force by the whole population

00:02:06.444 --> 00:02:07.427
aged 15 and over.

00:02:08.577 --> 00:02:12.190
A provisional figure rests on the
data available at the time

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:13.093
of the release.

00:02:13.843 --> 00:02:17.589
The office revises the figure when
the round is complete, and the

00:02:17.589 --> 00:02:20.643
release calendar gives the date of
the final figures.

00:02:21.793 --> 00:02:25.507
The release for the second quarter
reports four headline figures.

00:02:26.257 --> 00:02:30.193
The unemployment rate was 6.1
percent, provisional, after 6.3

00:02:30.193 --> 00:02:32.064
percent in the first quarter.

00:02:32.814 --> 00:02:37.275
Youth unemployment, for people aged
15 to 24, was 16.8 percent,

00:02:37.275 --> 00:02:38.620
after 17.5 percent.

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:43.256
Labour force participation rose to 61.2 percent, after 60.9 percent.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:50.229
The number of employed persons was
4,312 thousand, after 4,268 thousand

00:02:50.229 --> 00:02:52.070
in the first quarter.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.515
The release reports these figures
with their definitions.

00:02:56.515 --> 00:03:00.042
The report on the labour market
discusses the reasons for the

00:03:00.042 --> 00:03:03.106
changes, with the judgement of the
office's analysts.

00:03:03.856 --> 00:03:07.623
Comparisons with earlier years, with
other countries, or with the

00:03:07.623 --> 00:03:11.332
administrative registers of
jobseekers need the same definitions

00:03:11.332 --> 00:03:12.723
and the same population.

00:03:12.723 --> 00:03:16.085
The methodology note of the survey
describes them in full.

00:03:16.835 --> 00:03:20.929
Before publication, a member of the
office's staff checked every figure

00:03:20.929 --> 00:03:23.235
in this video against the release
table.

00:03:24.585 --> 00:03:28.962
The release table, the report on the
labour market, and the transcript of

00:03:28.962 --> 00:03:30.881
this video are at stats.example.
